# Maintainer: local dipper port
# Based on Nura pmaports device/community/linux-postmarketos-qcom-sdm845
# (pkgver 7.1_rc1, pkgrel 4, kernel sdm845/7.1-dev).
#
# Differences from the upstream package:
#   * adds arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm845-xiaomi-dipper.dts
#   * builds drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-samsung-ea8074.c into the kernel
#     (obj-y, display must own MDSS early - see docs/HARDWARE-NOTES.md)
#   * config disables CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF / _MODULES: pahole 1.32 segfaults
#     while generating BTF inside the WSL2 Alpine chroot (kernel suggests
#     "Try to disable CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF"). BTF is debug-only, no runtime impact.
#   * everything else (config, toolchain, install) is identical to upstream
maintainer="dipper local port <dipper@example.com>"
_flavor="postmarketos-qcom-sdm845-dipper"
pkgname=linux-$_flavor
pkgver=7.1_rc1
# r1: fix EA8074 init sequence writing DCS 0x51 brightness=0 (screen went
#     black right after the kernel penguin); set max brightness 1023 instead.
# r2: disable PMI8998 LAB/IBB per downstream dipper-p0.dtsi and drop the
#     panel's vddpos/vddneg supplies. On dipper the beryllium lab/ibb config
#     fires irq 87 and NULL-derefs in qcom_labibb_ocp_isr() at 0.226s, which
#     breaks panel supplies and is followed by DSI PLL lock failures.
# r3: add disp_vddio_vreg (regulator-fixed on TLMM GPIO 23) and route the
#     panel's vddio through it, per downstream dipper-p0.dtsi:99. Hardware
#     test showed DSI clocks locked + DPU scanning 3980 frames while the
#     screen stayed completely black -> display I/O rail was never powered.
# r4: panel driver now uses MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO_BURST (downstream
#     qcom,mdss-dsi-traffic-mode = "burst_mode"), same as panel-samsung-ea8076.c.
#     Pure command mode gave the DPU no frame-push path: clocks locked and the
#     DPU scanned 3980 frames, but nothing reached the panel.
# r5: revert to command mode (r4 VIDEO_BURST did not light the panel either)
#     and add DCS read-back diagnostics (GET_POWER_MODE/DISPLAY_MODE/PIXEL_
#     FORMAT) in prepare() to tell "init never reached the panel" from
#     "panel is on but DPU does not push pixels".
# r6: the DCS read-back blocked the whole initramfs (read channel never
#     answers), so it is removed. Instead the post-exit-sleep delay is
#     raised from the vendor's 10 ms to the MIPI-required 120 ms: with only
#     10 ms the following 0x29 (set display on) is silently dropped.
# r7: THE missing rail. Vendor dsi_amoled_panel_pwr_supply has TWO entries:
#     vddio 1.8 V AND vci 3.0 V; panel node uses vci-supply = <&disp_vci_vreg>
#     which is a regulator-fixed on TLMM GPIO 35 (dipper-p0.dtsi:90).
#     Only vddio was wired, so the panel was never powered - matching every
#     observed symptom (DSI locked, DPU scanning, fb0 has content, black screen).
# r8: retry MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO_BURST now that vci 3.0 V is actually present.
#     The r4 burst experiment was invalid (panel unpowered back then). Vendor
#     mdss_dsi.c drives cmd-mode panels with burst traffic + a first-frame
#     kickoff (mdss_dsi_cmdlist_commit(ctrl, 1)); burst mode is mainline's
#     equivalent path that pushes frames without an explicit kickoff.
# r9: force video mode (MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO | VIDEO_BURST). With r7/r8 the
#     panel had both rails at the right voltage and DPU reported
#     total_framecount increasing, yet the panel stayed dark, so try the
#     continuous-scanout path instead of cmd-mode kickoff.
# r10: video mode kept, but porches widened to 64/20/64 (EA8076 style).
#      The downstream 56/18/56 blanking gave only 4 fps in video mode.
# r11: back to cmd mode (video mode is dead): r10 boot took 179 s to reach
#      the debug shell. Root cause found in dpu_hw_intf_enable_te(): it
#      hard-codes INTF_TEAR_TEAR_CHECK_EN=1, so the DPU waits forever for a
#      TE event that mainline never delivers (panel TE is TLMM GPIO 10 and
#      dsi_host.c has no TE handling). Force that bit to 0 so the DPU pushes
#      frames on its internal vsync cadence.
# r12: tearcheck disable confirmed applied but screen still black, so add a
#      DSI register dump in msm_dsi_host_enable() (STATUS0 / FIFO / ACK_ERR /
#      TIMEOUT / EOT / CLK) to find out whether the DSI controller is
#      actually pushing pixels.
# r13: the first dump fired at 5.50 s, before the panel was on (5.78 s), so
#      STATUS0=0 was meaningless. Move the dump into a delayed work that
#      sleeps 3 s after host enable.
# r14: r13 queued the 3 s sleep on the DSI ordered workqueue and deadlocked
#      init (gadget never enumerated). Use schedule_delayed_work() on the
#      system wq instead, so the DSI queue and err_work stay responsive.
# r15: r11 only patched INTF tear-check, but sdm845 has DPU 4.0 and
#      dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_init() leaves has_intf_te=false, so the real
#      tear-check is on the PINGPONG block (dpu_hw_pingpong.c:105
#      PP_TEAR_CHECK_EN=1) and the INTF patch never ran. Disable PP
#      tear-check, enable PP autorefresh, stop disable_autorefresh()
#      on every kickoff, and replace the single-point DSI dump with
#      50 x 20 ms STATUS0 sampling on the system workqueue.
# r16: r15 made the DSI cmd engines busy (STATUS0=0x5) but the panel
#      still stayed black -> cmd-mode GRAM/TE refresh is the remaining
#      suspect. Retry video mode now that PP tear-check is disabled
#      (r9/r10 stalls were caused by the still-enabled tear-check).
#      Injected via sed on the copied panel driver, so sha512sums stay.
# r19: r16's video mode was pointless - the vendor dtsi says this panel is
#      dsi_cmd_mode and video streams are simply not accepted. Back to cmd
#      mode, and instead of guessing further call the tree's unused
#      msm_dsi_host_test_pattern_en() right after host enable: it writes a
#      checkerboard into panel GRAM without the DPU, so the next boot
#      separates 'panel/link dead' from 'DPU content wrong'.
# r20: ROOT CAUSE. reset-gpios is GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW, but
#      samsung_ea8074_reset() copied downstream's physical sequence
#      <1 1>,<0 1>,<1 10> verbatim. Logically that is assert -> release
#      1 ms -> assert, so the panel stayed in reset for the entire init
#      sequence. Writes are fire-and-forget, which is why every round
#      looked 'healthy' while the DCS read channel never answered and the
#      screen stayed black. Reset is now expressed in gpiod terms
#      (0 = released / physical high), and the r19 TPG is dropped.
# r21: panel is lit, so the r15 50 x 20 ms STATUS0 sampler is pure log
#      noise - drop it (dump_work, its struct member, INIT and schedule).
#      PP tear-check / autorefresh patches stay: TE routing does not exist
#      yet. The panel driver now runs a one-shot DCS read 3 s after
#      prepare() to prove the read channel really answers; it runs in a
#      delayed work so a wedged read can never block boot again.
# r22: real TE. sdm845 does not route the panel TE through the DSI
#      controller - TLMM GPIO 10 is muxed as "mdp_vsync" straight into the
#      display processor and picked up as DPU_VSYNC_SOURCE_GPIO_0. With that
#      pinctrl state plus qcom,te-source in the DTS, the DPU tear-check can
#      finally follow the panel, so the r11 INTF sed and the whole r15
#      heredoc (PP_TEAR_CHECK_EN=0 / autorefresh / enable_te no-op) are
#      removed and upstream tear-check behaviour is restored.
# r23: touch. The FTS5 controller streams one record per slot on every scan
#      (~37 Hz) with pressure 0 for every slot that is not touched; the old
#      "zero pressure" workaround turned those records into ten permanent
#      fingers, which made every tap and gesture disappear. Idle records are
#      now dropped, stmfts5_parse_events() no longer aborts the whole batch on
#      a status record, and accepted contacts log one line to dmesg.
# r24: touch, part 2. stmfts_irq_handler() reads the whole 256-byte event
#      stack on every scan (~37 Hz) while the bus was still at its 100 kHz
#      default, so a single read took ~23 ms against a ~27 ms scan period.
#      The chip's buffer backed up, the controller stretched the clock and the
#      GENI/GPI engine wedged ("CH STOP completion timeout" -> every later
#      transfer -110) about 5.5 minutes into every boot. The dipper touch bus
#      now runs at 400 kHz (clock-frequency on &i2c14).
# r25: touch, part 3 - and the C patches move to real .patch files.
#      (a) The 5-minute bus wedge is the GPI DMA engine, not the chip:
#          geni_i2c only takes the GPI path for messages >= 32 B and stmfts
#          reads 256 B per interrupt, so the touch bus is a continuous DMA
#          stream. geni_i2c now honours a "qcom,no-dma" DT property (sdm845
#          could only set no_dma from platform data) and &i2c14 uses it.
#      (b) stmfts pulses the touch reset GPIO after 5 consecutive read errors
#          (self-heal) and dumps two raw records every 128 interrupts, so we
#          can see what a real finger produces without racing /dev/i2c-14.
#      (c) r23's inline heredoc is replaced by stmfts-touch.patch, which
#          carries both the r23 pressure fix and the r25 additions.
# r26: touch, part 4 - split the event read into two single-message transfers.
#      geni_i2c takes the Qualcomm multi-descriptor GPI path for num >= 2 and
#      that path is what deadlocks after a few minutes ('cmd: CH STOP
#      completion timeout'). The FTS controller latches the read command, so
#      a STOP between command and read is fine (verified 20/20 on the device).
#      Also records that qcom,no-dma is a no-op on this SE (FIFO disabled).
# r27: touch, part 5 - move touch off the GENI SE onto i2c-gpio, and use the
#      vendor two-byte sense-on command.
#      (a) a98000.i2c has its FIFO disabled in hardware, so geni_i2c must move
#          every transfer through the GPI DMA engine (which is why qcom,no-dma
#          did nothing in r25). That engine deadlocks after a few hundred touch
#          reads and stays dead until reboot. GPIO 33/34 are the same two wires
#          (qup14) - r27 muxes them back to GPIO and runs a bit-banged i2c-gpio
#          bus at ~100 kHz with the FTS5 controller on it.
#      (b) the stock driver sends {0x93, 0x01} as sense-on, not a bare 0x93 or a
#          scan-mode write.
#      CONFIG_I2C_GPIO becomes =y.
# r28: touch diagnostics + SDA/SCL swap on the i2c-gpio bus.
#      With r27 the stmfts probe failed with -ENXIO and printed nothing, which
#      looks exactly like 'driver never tried'. The bus now logs the failure
#      (and the first record on success). GPIO 33/34 are also swapped, since
#      the geni pin order does not tell us which wire is SDA.
# r29: put the event read back to a single two-message transfer.
#      r26 split it into write-then-read to dodge the geni multi-descriptor
#      path, but on i2c-gpio the read must stay in the same transaction with a
#      repeated start: the split version made every read fail with -EIO (the
#      controller had no command latched). r27 already moved the bus off geni,
#      so the original combined transfer is both correct and safe here.
# r30: touch VDD back to disp_vddio_vreg (GPIO23). vendor dipper-p0.dtsi:546
#      wires touch vdd to disp_vddio_vreg and avdd to pm8998_l28; the port
#      has been feeding vdd from L14, which lets I2C work but leaves the
#      controller returning all-zero event data.
# r31: use the REAL FTS5 pointer format from the stock driver.
#      The stock fts_ts driver (fts.c fts_enter_pointer_event_handler +
#      ftsSoftware.h) uses event ids 0x03 enter / 0x04 leave / 0x05 motion,
#      touch id in the LOW nibble of event[1] (high nibble = touch count),
#      x = (e[2]<<4)|((e[4]&0xF0)>>4), y = (e[3]<<4)|(e[4]&0x0F) and
#      pressure = e[5]&0x3F. mainline's 0x13/0x23/0x33 guess with the id in
#      the high nibble is a different protocol - that is why this controller
#      never reported a single touch. Also touchscreen-size-y -> 2160, the
#      real touch area (y vendor X/Y_AXIS_MAX in fts.h).
# r32: port the stock driver bring-up sequence (readChipInfo + IER + A5 + sense on).
#      The controller has been ACKing I2C but returning empty event records, which
#      points at a controller that was never initialised: mainline only reads the
#      FIFO once.  stmfts5_configure() now follows the stock fts_ts path -
#      chip info block from the framebuffer (0xD0 @0x8000), hardware register
#      reset (0xB6 @0x0023), CONTROL_READY (0x10) wait, IER write (0x001C=0x41)
#      with read back so the bus read path is proven, full initialisation (0xA5)
#      and sense on (0x93 0x01) - and logs every step so the failure point is
#      visible in dmesg.
# r33: command/response matrix for the FTS controller.
#      r32 showed the controller ACKs every write and returns one 03 event
#      right after 0xA5, but all register reads come back zero - so the bus
#      works and the chip is listening.  This walks the stock init commands
#      (sleep-out, sense-on, full-init, force-cal, cx-tuning, scan mode, IER)
#      one at a time and logs every event each one produces, which pins down
#      how far the controller's scanner actually gets.
# r34: power cycle the analog rail and retry the reset before giving up.
#      r32/r33 proved the controller ACKs writes but ignores every command and
#      returns zero for all registers and events, which is what an FTS part
#      looks like while it sits in its bootloader.  A reset pulse alone does not
#      get it out of there; the stock driver's fts_chip_powercycle() cuts the
#      supplies instead.  ldo28 (avdd) feeds the touch controller alone, so it
#      can be cycled without touching the panel.  Also retries the reset once
#      with a 300 ms pulse if CONTROL_READY does not show up.
# r35: touch back on GENI (a98000.i2c at 400 kHz, qcom,no-dma) with the
#      vendor bring-up.  r23-r26 read real records through this bus, so
#      this run separates 'controller is alive, bit-banged bus is at fault'
#      from 'controller itself stopped answering'.
# r36: make the bring-up tunable from module parameters.
#      The controller no longer runs its firmware: two different buses (GENI and
#      i2c-gpio), a 100 ms reset and an avdd power cycle all give the same
#      answer - I2C ACK, everything else zero.  pwrcycle, reset_ms and
#      settle_ms let the power/reset combination be re-tried with a simple
#      'modprobe stmfts ...' instead of a new kernel each time.
# r37: keep the interrupt masked when the controller never reports CONTROL_READY.
#      With no firmware the controller holds its IRQ line asserted, so the
#      driver was draining a 256 byte transfer at every interrupt for nothing
#      (and on GENI that GPI traffic is what deadlocks after a few minutes).
#      The bring-up still runs and logs everything, but the system stays quiet
#      until a working controller (firmware) is available.
# r38: touch back on i2c-gpio (GPIO33/34).  The controller firmware now
#      runs; GENI DMA errors during the initramfs handoff raced the USB
#      gadget teardown into a NULL deref panic.  i2c-gpio has no DMA.
# r39: non-FTM3 registers + vendor power sequence: reset 0x0028=0x80
#      (was 0x0023=0x01), IER 0x002c (was 0x001c), avdd->2ms->vdd
#      power-on, no duplicate GPIO reset in configure().
# r40: FTS5 helper placement fix on top of r39 (non-FTM3 registers and power sequence).
# r41: fix patch application order (power_on before helpers) and use non-FTM3 registers; place helpers before configure.
# r42: do not mask the IRQ when CONTROL_READY(0x10) is missing. The stock
#      fts_init() tolerates that timeout and still installs/enables the IRQ
#      in fts_init_hw(); this controller answers with 0x03 events instead.
# r42: do not mask the IRQ when CONTROL_READY(0x10) is missing. The stock
#      fts_init() tolerates that timeout and still enables the IRQ in
#      fts_init_hw(); this controller answers with 0x03 events instead.
# r43: touch back on GENI &i2c14 (stock Android bus). i2c-gpio
#      ACKs but reads back all zeros even with valid supplies.
# r45: let reset_ms=0 skip the power_on GPIO pulse so the firmware loaded
#      at cold power-on is preserved (test of the firmware-timing hypothesis).
# r45: reset_ms=0 skips the power_on GPIO pulse to preserve cold-loaded firmware.
# r45: reset_ms=0 skips the power_on GPIO pulse to preserve cold-loaded firmware.
# r46: ldo28 (touch avdd) from LPM to HPM so the firmware can boot.
# r47: upload the stock FTS firmware from the kernel when CONTROL_READY never
#      arrives. Android does exactly this (fts_fw_update_auto ->
#      fts_flash_procedure -> flashProcedure with the embedded fts_fw.h
#      array); postmarketOS never did, so a controller with an empty or
#      wrong flash only ACKed on I2C and returned zero bytes for the
#      framebuffer and the event FIFO.
# r47: upload the stock FTS firmware from the kernel when CONTROL_READY never arrives (same as Android's fts_fw_update_auto).
# r47: upload the stock FTS firmware from the kernel when CONTROL_READY never arrives.
# r48: flash_burn parity (drop the extra warm-boot write) + power-cycle retry
#      and a 3 s CONTROL_READY window after the firmware upload.
# r48: match flash_burn (no warm boot) + power-cycle retry, 3 s CONTROL_READY.
# r49: GENI DMA back on (Android uses it; PIO mode returned all-zero reads),
#      and the built-in firmware upload is now opt-in (wrong variant).
# r49: GENI DMA re-enabled (stock driver uses it; PIO returned zero reads) and
#      the firmware upload is now opt-in: Android shows the chip already runs
#      0x0054 and is never reflashed, and our blob was a different variant.
# r49: GENI DMA back on, firmware upload opt-in.
# r50: reset released during power-up (GPIOD_OUT_LOW) like the stock driver.
# r51: assert reset, cycle rails, release reset so on-chip firmware boots.
# r52: touch avdd back to the stock 3.3 V (L28). At 2.856 V the FTS firmware
#      never boots: the chip ACKs I2C but every read returns 0.
# r53: L28 keeps its safe range and the touch driver raises avdd to the
#      stock 3.3 V itself (fixed 3.3 V in DT crashes RPMh).
# r53: raise touch avdd to 3.3 V from the driver instead of pinning L28 in DT.
# r54: avdd to 3.312 V (top 8 mV step); 3.300 V is not a valid PM8998 level.
# r54: avdd to 3.312 V (top 8 mV step).
# r54: avdd to 3.312 V.
# r54: avdd to 3.312 V.
# r55: raise avdd (supplies[1], L28) to 3312 mV - the previous attempt hit vdd.
# r56: avdd voltage change is opt-in (avdd_uv module param); doing it at probe deadlocks RPMh.
# r57: apply avdd voltage after the rail is enabled and log the real voltage.
# r58: pin L28 at 3312 mV (top valid step) instead of 2856-3312 mV; the
#      controller's avdd must match the stock 3.3 V for its firmware to boot.
# r59: L28 pinned at 3312 mV with stock-style LPM initial mode.
# r60: L28 at the stock 3.300 V, no forced HPM.
# r61: correct firmware (chip 36,39 / fw 0x0054) embedded; DTS back to safe L28 range.
# r62: L28 without forced HPM mode request.
# r63: keep L28 as XBL configured it; touch uses a fixed 3.3 V supply.
# r64: dipper touch pinctrl with bias-pull-up on IRQ31/RESET32.
# r65: vendor-style aggregated RPMh request for L28 (opt-in) and GPIO reset before chip info.
# r66: touch back to vendor behaviour: real L28 avdd, reset held asserted
#      during power-up, one reset only, 500 ms CONTROL_READY polling with
#      3 retries, 144-byte chip-info read with 0xA5 check.
# r67: aggregated RPMh request now owns the avdd rail (no disable/enable afterwards)\n#      and rails are only disabled when actually enabled.
# r68: change avdd after the rails are off and the RPMh TCS has drained\n#      (Android logs show TCS-busy retries; mainline has none).
# r69: aggregate voltage+enable into one RPMh message (vendor 4.9 behaviour)\n#      and pin L28 at the stock 3.300 V so the FTS firmware can boot.
# r70: opt-in voltage+enable aggregation via qcom,init-voltage (L28 only).
# r70: opt-in voltage+enable aggregation via qcom,init-voltage (L28 only).
# r71: send the init-voltage request asynchronously so a busy TCS cannot stall\n#      RPMh before the root filesystem is mounted.
# r72: touch vddio moved to the always-on PM8998 L14 (display-independent)
#      and L28 back to its 2.856-3.312 V range (2.856 V is sufficient).
# r73: pure mainline stmfts driver (vendor-sequence changes removed) with
#      touch vddio on the always-on PM8998 L14.
# r74: FTS5 events are read one at a time (0x85); the old 0x86 bulk read
#      left garbage in the buffer and made the cursor jump.
# r74: read FTS5 events one at a time (0x85) to stop the cursor jumping.
# r75: read one FTS5 event per 0x85 via i2c_transfer (SMBus block added a length byte).
# r76: 0x86 limited to one 8-byte event (bulk read appended garbage).
# r76: 0x86 read limited to one 8-byte event.
# r77: touch abs range 4096x4096 (controller reports 12-bit raw values).
# r77: touch abs range 4096x4096 (12-bit controller values).
# r78: touch vdd = dedicated GPIO23 1.8 V fixed regulator, 1080x2248, no pinctrl
#      (matches the working Mi 8 mainline DT), and the stock stmfts driver.
# r79: enable qupv3_id_1 + gpi_dma0/1 (real DMA for i2c14); drop both
#      patches so this is the stock stmfts driver plus the reference DT.
# r80: touch node copied verbatim from the working mainline DT.
# r81: panel vddio on L14 like the reference DT; GPIO 23 now belongs only to
#      the touch controller (both regulators were claiming it).
# r82: DTS replaced with the working Mi 8 mainline DT (Mechres), unmodified,
#      to verify touch before merging WiFi/modem back in.
# r83: explicit gpio31/32 pinctrl so QUP11 cannot keep the touch IRQ/reset muxed.
# r84: enable &gpu_zap_shader (Adreno zap shader) -- GPU was running degraded.
# r85: real fix for the touch controller.  Its FIFO speaks the classic ST event
#      format (vendor fts.c), not the FTM5 one mainline's stmfts5 parser
#      decodes, so slot id / X / Y were assembled from the wrong bytes -- that
#      is what made the pointer jump and lag.  stmfts-touch.patch now:
#        - drains the FIFO the way the vendor does (0x85 -> queue depth in the
#          low 5 bits of byte 7 -> 0x86 for exactly that many events), instead
#          of reading a fixed 256 bytes of mostly stale data;
#        - decodes X/Y/pressure/slot from the classic layout;
#        - syncs input once per interrupt instead of once per event.
#      The touch node also declares the controller's real Y range (2160).
# r86: the r85 parser was a wrong guess -- this controller really does speak
#      FTS5 (see the reference bring-up log), and 0x85/FIFO-depth did not drain
#      the queue at all: the IRQ line stayed asserted and fired 170k times while
#      every read came back zero.  Back to the stock 0x86 + 256 byte read and the
#      stock FTS5 parser, plus two module parameters so the real byte layout and
#      the scan-mode value can be checked without another rebuild:
#        dump_batches=N  hexdump the raw FIFO of the first N interrupts
#        scan_mode=VAL   value sent with 0xa0 (0 disables the write)
# r87: the controller never reported a contact because nothing ever armed it.
#      Downstream does three things mainline's FTS5 path does not, and all three
#      are now in stmfts-touch.patch:
#        - HW register 0x002c (interrupt sources) gets 0x41, mainline wrote 0x01,
#          which leaves the touch event source masked;
#        - "sense on without calibration" is the two byte command 0x93 0x01 (the
#          vendor comments out the plain one byte form), sent at probe and after
#          every wake-up;
#        - 0xa0 is not a command this part knows, it is only written when
#          scan_mode is set explicitly (and it makes the chip stream ten bogus
#          zero-pressure motion slots).
# r89: touch finally explained.  The controller runs host-loaded firmware:
#      the downstream driver always flashes st_fts_e1.ftb at probe (keeping the
#      CX calibration pages), and without it the part ACKs I2C but never starts
#      -- every register readback is zero and no interrupt ever fires.  That is
#      why the panel worked after a warm reboot (firmware still in RAM from
#      Android) and went silent after the first real power cycle.
#      stmfts-touch.patch now: probes CONTROL_READY after reset, flashes the
#      firmware from /lib/firmware when it is not alive (warm boot, flash
#      unlock, page erase with CX kept, DMA fill of code+config, reset), then
#      arms IER=0x41 and sends the 0x93 0x01 sense-on pair.
# r101: sense-on 必须是 {0xa0, 0x00, 0x01}（多指扫描）。
#       0xff 会打开额外的扫描模式，正是把 Mi 8 触摸弄成幽灵触控的原因；
#       上游 v6 与实测均确认 0x01 正确。arm 顺序：CONTROL_READY -> IER=0x41 -> sense-on。
#       改这段前先读 docs/PROGRESS.md，别再按 0xff/0x93 试。
